Compilation:
```
arm-none-eabi-gcc \
    -mcpu=cortex-m4 \
    -mthumb \
    -mfloat-abi=soft \
    -ffreestanding \
    -fno-builtin \
    -ffunction-sections \
    -fdata-sections \
    -g3 -O0 \
    -Wall -Wextra \
    -nostdlib \
    -T linker/tm4c123gh6pm.ld \
    -Wl,--gc-sections \
    -Wl,-Map=build/firmware.map \
    startup/startup.S src/main.c src/morse_LED.c\
    -o build/firmware.elf -I include
```

flash onto MCU:
```
openocd -f board/ek-tm4c123gxl.cfg \
    -c "program build/firmware.elf verify reset exit"
```

DEBUGGING:

Prerequisite: install arm-none-eabi-gdb. Connect board to mac. 

1. Have OpenOCD start the gdb server on the board. This is typically done on port 3333
```
openocd -f board/ti_ek-tm4c123gxl.cfg
```
2. In another terminal, start the gdb and load the debuggign info compiled into the .eld file:
```
arm-none-eabi-gdb build/firmware.elf
```
3. Inside the gdb, connect to the OpenOCD server on the board. This allows you to communicate with your processor!
```
target extended-remote localhost:3333
```

Useful commands:
break <function>
monitor reset halt : reset the processor and pause it
backtrace : show the current call stack
x/1tw <address> : allows you to examine the 32 bit value at a memory address. 
- syntax: x/[count][Format][UnitSize]
- count: how many units of memory to display
- Format: how to interpret and print the data (x: hexadecimal, d: signed decimal, t: binary, c:Character. s: null terminated string)
- Unit size: The size of each unit of memory to read (b : byte, w: word (4 bytes), g: giant word (8 bytes))
